# Linux Users

A user is an individual account that can log into the Linux operating system.

Every user has:

- Username
- User ID (UID)
- Primary Group
- Home Directory
- Login Shell

Linux uses users to identify who is performing actions on the system.

---

# Linux Groups

A group is a collection of users.

Instead of assigning permissions individually to every user, Linux administrators assign permissions to groups.

Example:

```
Finance Group

├── John
├── Sarah
├── David
```

All members of the Finance group receive the permissions assigned to that group.

This simplifies administration.

---

# Why Groups are Important

Without groups:

Administrator must assign permissions to every individual user.

With groups:

Administrator only assigns permissions once to the group.

Any authorized user added to that group automatically receives the appropriate access.

---

# Linux Commands Learned

## Current Logged-in User

```bash
whoami
```

Displays the username of the currently logged-in user.

---

## User Information

```bash
id
```

Displays:

- Username
- User ID (UID)
- Group ID (GID)
- Supplementary Groups

---

## View User Groups

```bash
groups
```

Displays all groups that the current user belongs to.

---

## View Local Users

```bash
cat /etc/passwd
```

Displays information about local user accounts.

---

## Create a Group

```bash
sudo groupadd it-support
```

Creates a new group.

---

## Create a User

```bash
sudo useradd -m supportuser
```

Creates a new user with a home directory.

---

## Set Password

```bash
sudo passwd supportuser
```

Assigns a password to the user.

---

## Add User to Group

```bash
sudo usermod -aG it-support supportuser
```

Adds the user to the specified supplementary group without removing existing group memberships.

---

## Verify Group Membership

```bash
id supportuser
```

or

```bash
groups supportuser
```

Confirms that the user belongs to the required group.

---

## Remove Practice User

```bash
sudo userdel -r supportuser
```

Removes the user and home directory.

---

## Remove Practice Group

```bash
sudo groupdel it-support
```

Deletes the group.

---

# Relationship Between Users, Groups, Ownership and Permissions

```
User

↓

Group

↓

Ownership

↓

Permissions

↓

Access
```

Linux uses all four components together to determine whether a user can access a file or directory.
